WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.570
An Austrian soldier had to rise to his feet to load his rifle; the Prussian facing him, lying flat on the ground, fired five rounds in the same span of time.

2
00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:14.980
That morning the fate of two empires rested on which end of the barrel a gun was loaded from.

3
00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:18.950
And by evening a new giant had been born in the middle of Europe.

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:37.840
In February of eighteen sixty-six, in the headquarters at Verona in northern Italy, a sixty-one-year-old commander sat looking for a way to turn down the greatest promotion of his life.

5
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:43.570
His name was Ludwig von Benedek, and he was the most beloved soldier in the Austrian Empire.

6
00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:52.180
Vienna was offering him command of the Northern Army, to be assembled in Bohemia — command, in other words, of everything the empire possessed.

7
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.030
Benedek refused three times.

8
00:00:55.030 --> 00:01:01.880
His reason was not an excuse but a cold diagnosis: he knew neither that terrain nor that enemy.

9
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.290
To understand the weight of those words, you have to look at where the man came from.

10
00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:15.580
Benedek was born on the fourteenth of July, eighteen oh four, in the town of Sopron in western Hungary, the son of a provincial physician.

11
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:24.990
He was Protestant, of Hungarian descent, and within an officer class defined by commanders who carried the title of count, he remained an outsider all his life.

12
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:33.280
He owed his rise not to family connections but to his courage, and to the strange, almost familial devotion his soldiers felt for him.

13
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:41.570
Among the rank and file he was a legend: a commander who spoke plainly, who stood beside his men in the trenches, who despised paperwork.

14
00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.020
And that entire life had been spent in a single geography: Italy.

15
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:52.070
He knew every ditch of the Lombard plain, every crossing of the river Mincio, every hill around Verona.

16
00:01:52.070 --> 00:02:04.360
On the twenty-fourth of June, eighteen fifty-nine, at Solferino, as the imperial army's center dissolved before the French, Benedek held the right wing on the ridges of San Martino against the Piedmontese all day long.

17
00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:08.170
The battle was lost, but on his own front he was not beaten.

18
00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:15.500
He returned to Vienna as "the Lion of Solferino" — the one bright name to emerge from a catastrophic decade for the empire.

19
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.030
And that was precisely the problem.

20
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:23.760
What Austria needed in eighteen sixty-six was not a symbol but a mind that knew the north.

21
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.090
Friction with Prussia had been escalating for months.

22
00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:37.460
Two years earlier the two states had fought shoulder to shoulder against Denmark, taking the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein together, and had then failed to agree on how to divide the spoils.

23
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:46.070
In Berlin there was a minister-president named Otto von Bismarck, and he was saying openly that a two-headed arrangement in the German world could not be sustained.

24
00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:54.520
Vienna's real fear was a war on two fronts: in the south the Kingdom of Italy wanted Venetia; in the north Prussia could march into Bohemia.

25
00:02:54.520 --> 00:03:00.890
The logic at the decision table ran like this: put your best commander where the danger is greatest.

26
00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:07.420
And so Benedek was taken out of Italy and sent to a country where he had never once campaigned.

27
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:14.590
Emperor Franz Joseph insisted; the commander who had written refusal after refusal finally bowed.

28
00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:20.640
He delayed his departure for weeks, but reached Vienna on the twelfth of May, eighteen sixty-six.

29
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:31.970
Archduke Albrecht, sent to the Italian front in his place, would win a brilliant victory there two months later at Custoza — which is to say Austria had placed both of its commanders on the wrong front.

30
00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:41.700
It is said that when Benedek arrived at his headquarters, he shut himself in with maps of Bohemia, trying until past midnight to memorize the names of villages he did not know.

31
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:50.150
On paper the army in his hands was enormous: seven corps, a powerful mass of cavalry, and the finest artillery in Europe.

32
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:53.160
But this army had no staff tradition.

33
00:03:53.160 --> 00:04:00.330
The corps commanders were archdukes and counts, unaccustomed to taking orders, and they did not speak to one another.

34
00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:09.900
Benedek's own chief of staff, Gideon von Krismanić, was a man who never fully explained the war plan to his commander, who carried that plan around like a secret.

35
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:16.750
So the empire had sent a commander into terrain he did not know — and made his own staff a stranger to him as well.

36
00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.760
In mid-June the war began.

37
00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:27.410
In the assembly of the German Confederation, mobilization against Prussia was put to a vote on Austria's motion;

38
00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:34.420
Prussia declared the confederation dissolved and on the fifteenth and sixteenth of June marched into the neighboring German states.

39
00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:42.390
What followed was that short, sharp conflict that would go down in history as the Seven Weeks' War — the one that redrew Europe.

40
00:04:42.390 --> 00:04:49.880
As Benedek dragged his army from Moravia toward Bohemia, he was aware that he was walking into a sentence he had written himself.

41
00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:58.650
The heaviest thing that can happen to a commander is not defeat; it is having said in advance, in writing, that he would lose — and being sent anyway.

42
00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:06.460
On the other side, in Berlin, there was a sixty-five-year-old man: frail, silent, very nearly invisible.

43
00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:14.590
Helmuth von Moltke had been chief of the Prussian General Staff since eighteen fifty-seven, and to that day he had never commanded an army in war.

44
00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:20.320
The place where he had proven his military gift was not the battlefield but the desk.

45
00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.890
Moltke's achievement was to change the very technology with which the art of war was practised.

46
00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:31.940
In the middle of the nineteenth century two new instruments had appeared: the railway and the telegraph.

47
00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:37.510
The railway could carry an army to the frontier in a matter of days instead of marching it for weeks.

48
00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:42.600
The telegraph made it possible to issue orders from hundreds of kilometres away.

49
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:47.850
Moltke fused these two instruments with an institution: the general staff.

50
00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:56.140
Officers who had passed through the same training, who spoke the same language and thought in the same way, were distributed among armies far apart from one another,

51
00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:02.670
so that the commander at the top did not have to spell out every detail — they all worked towards the same objective.

52
00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:05.840
The figures lay the difference bare.

53
00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.250
Prussia had five railway lines reaching towards the theatre of war; Austria had one.

54
00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:18.100
Prussia completed its mobilisation within weeks, while Austrian troops were still swallowing dust in marching columns.

55
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.470
And then there was the rifle.

56
00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:29.080
Prussian infantry carried the breech-loading Dreyse needle gun: a soldier could lie flat on the ground, behind cover, and fire four or five rounds a minute.

57
00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:37.370
Austrian infantry, with the muzzle-loading Lorenz rifle, had to stand upright and tip the barrel skyward, and could barely manage two.

58
00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:46.140
Austria had misread the lesson it learned from the French in eighteen fifty-nine, and had adopted a doctrine resting not on firepower but on the bayonet charge.

59
00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:51.230
That meant attacking in close order, at a run, trying to close chest to chest.

60
00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:58.400
Against that, Austrian artillery was better than Prussia's; the rifled Austrian guns shot further and more accurately.

61
00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:03.330
The war would come down to the question of on what ground these two advantages would meet.

62
00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.180
Moltke divided his army into three.

63
00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:17.510
On the left wing, Herwarth von Bittenfeld's Army of the Elbe, roughly thirty-nine thousand strong; in the centre, Prince Friedrich Karl's First Army of eighty-five thousand; and far off on the right,

64
00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:21.800
Crown Prince Friedrich Wilhelm's Second Army of one hundred thousand.

65
00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:26.410
The three armies were strung out along an arc of some four hundred kilometres.

66
00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:33.580
To the classical military mind this was suicide: the enemy could push into the gaps and crush the armies one by one.

67
00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:41.230
Moltke's principle was precisely the opposite, and it fitted into a single sentence: march divided, strike together.

68
00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:48.080
Marching divided meant speed and supply; striking together meant catching the enemy from the front and the flank at the same moment.

69
00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.490
The price of this principle was timing, and the price of timing was communication.

70
00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:57.780
At the end of June there was a series of clashes along the frontier.

71
00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:07.830
On the twenty-seventh of June, at Trautenau, Ludwig von Gablenz's Austrian corps threw the Prussians back; it was the only Austrian victory of the entire campaign,

72
00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:12.760
and Austria paid for it with several times the Prussian casualties.

73
00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:19.130
In those same days, at Nachod, at Skalitz, at Gitschin, the needle gun showed what it could do.

74
00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:25.820
Austrian divisions ran up the slopes in dense columns and melted away in front of Prussians firing from the prone.

75
00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:30.590
On the first of July, Benedek sent his emperor a telegram.

76
00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:36.800
He wrote that the army was being dragged towards catastrophe and begged that peace be made at any price.

77
00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:44.610
Franz Joseph's reply was short: peace was impossible; if a withdrawal could not be avoided, it must be carried out in good order.

78
00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:48.420
And he added a question — has a battle been fought?

79
00:08:48.420 --> 00:08:51.590
Benedek now had no choice but to fight.

80
00:08:51.590 --> 00:08:57.960
He placed his army on the hills north-west of Königgrätz, the fortress town on the bank of the Elbe.

81
00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:06.250
These high ridges put his guns in a commanding position, but behind them lay a river; if he were beaten, the army would be funnelled into a single narrow bottleneck.

82
00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.580
The battle would be given two names.

83
00:09:09.580 --> 00:09:18.830
The Prussians used the name of the fortress at their enemy's back; the Austrians and the French used the name of the small village in the middle of the front — Sadowa.

84
00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:28.080
On the night of the second of July, a cavalry patrol reported to Prussian headquarters that the Austrian army had not fled, that it was standing on the hills.

85
00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:38.770
When Moltke heard this, he knew the opportunity he had been waiting for had come: the First Army would hold the enemy from the front, while the Crown Prince's Second Army came down on the flank from the north.

86
00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:41.620
There was only one problem.

87
00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:47.670
The Crown Prince was forty kilometres away, and the telegraph line to him was not working that night.

88
00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.360
The order went on horseback.

89
00:09:50.360 --> 00:10:01.370
Among the aides who set out at midnight, Lieutenant Colonel Count Finckenstein crossed that distance at a gallop, through rain, through mud, through darkness, and delivered the order at four in the morning.

90
00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:07.100
The fate of the whole campaign hung, for a few hours, on the speed of a single rider.

91
00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:13.150
The morning of the third of July, eighteen sixty-six, opened with rain and a low mist.

92
00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:19.200
The fields were knee-deep in mud, the wheat was beaten flat, the streambeds were in flood.

93
00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:28.770
Towards eight o'clock the Prussian First Army came down to the Bistritz, the stream running through the middle of the front, and began to cross by the bridges around the village of Sadowa.

94
00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:35.940
Facing them, on the Lipa heights two or three kilometres beyond, hundreds of Austrian gun barrels were waiting.

95
00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:42.950
The moment the Prussians crossed the stream those guns began to speak, and they did not fall silent all morning.

96
00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:49.160
The Prussian centre was pinned on the open slope, under a fire its own guns could not reach.

97
00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:55.370
The real slaughter, though, took place on the right, in the woods at the northern end of the line.

98
00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:59.660
It was called the Swiepwald — the Swiep forest, for short.

99
00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:07.310
A few kilometers long, thick with old trees, a wood in which no one could see more than about twenty meters in any direction.

100
00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:15.120
The Prussian Seventh Division followed its commander, Eduard von Fransecky, into that forest, and never came out again.

101
00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:24.210
Benedek's order had been clear: the corps on the right wing were to hold their positions and keep the Horenowes heights against the Second Army, which might come down from the north.

102
00:11:24.210 --> 00:11:34.260
But the sound of rifle fire drifting out of the woods, and the sheer smallness of the Prussian division visible outside them, drew two corps commanders into an irresistible temptation.

103
00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:42.710
First Count Festetics's Fourth Corps, then Count Thun's Second Corps threw themselves forward against orders and plunged in among those trees.

104
00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:48.920
Two entire corps were spent to crush a single division, and the right wing was left empty.

105
00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:52.570
The forest stripped Austria of every advantage it had.

106
00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:57.820
The artillery could not fire; there was no room for a bayonet charge in close order.

107
00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:06.910
The only thing possible was to shoot between the tree trunks at fifteen paces, and that was precisely the range at which the needle gun was the finest weapon in the world.

108
00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:12.160
Austrian battalions advanced again and again, and again and again were thrown back.

109
00:12:12.160 --> 00:12:23.970
A counterattack led by Colonel Carl von Pöckh drove the Prussians back for a moment; then fire from a Prussian division coming in on the flank all but annihilated that battalion, and Pöckh was killed in the fighting.

110
00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:32.580
A fragment of a cannon shell tore off Count Festetics's foot; Anton von Mollinary, who took his place, went on pressing into the woods.

111
00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:36.070
Fransecky's division stayed in there for five hours.

112
00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:40.360
It had gone in with nine thousand men and lost close to two thousand.

113
00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.090
But it held.

114
00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:49.900
The other side paid far more dearly: by some accounts those two Austrian corps lost nearly ten thousand men over a single copse.

115
00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:57.870
Toward midday the Prussian headquarters, on the low rise called the Roskosberg, came face to face with an unpleasant truth.

116
00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:07.280
King Wilhelm the First was there, Moltke was there, and Bismarck was there too, moving about not in civilian dress but in the uniform of a reserve officer.

117
00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:14.450
The center could not advance, the right wing was bleeding inside a forest, and there was still no word from the Crown Prince.

118
00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:20.980
Shells were falling so close that Bismarck is said to have seized the king's bridle to pull him back out of the line of fire.

119
00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:26.870
A detail Bismarck later recounted conveys the tension of those hours better than any commander's report.

120
00:13:26.870 --> 00:13:29.720
He held out his cigar case to Moltke.

121
00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:34.010
Inside were one good cigar and a few ordinary ones.

122
00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:39.900
Moltke leaned over the case, looked without hurrying, picked out the best one and took it.

123
00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:47.390
In that instant Bismarck was reassured: if the man was still calm enough to make a choice, things could not be all that bad.

124
00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:49.760
And indeed they were not.

125
00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:55.970
The price the Prussian center had been paying all morning was, in fact, Moltke's plan working.

126
00:13:55.970 --> 00:14:01.700
Benedek's entire attention and all his reserves had been drawn to the front and to that forest.

127
00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:05.030
The door facing north stood wide open.

128
00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:09.800
Crown Prince Friedrich Wilhelm had been on the march since six o'clock that morning.

129
00:14:09.800 --> 00:14:20.810
He was thirty-four years old, a crown prince known for his liberal leanings, inwardly opposed to the policy his father was pursuing with Bismarck; on that day none of it mattered in the slightest.

130
00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:26.380
He had one hundred thousand men in hand, and kilometers of road that had to be pushed through the mud.

131
00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:32.750
Around half past two in the afternoon the vanguard of the Second Army appeared on the heights to the north.

132
00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:39.920
The Austrian right wing they should have found in front of them was not there; that wing had been fighting in the Swiep forest since morning.

133
00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:48.050
The Prussians climbed an utterly empty ridge line, and when they reached the crest they saw, spread out below them, the rear of the Austrian army.

134
00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:52.660
The key to the whole position was Chlum — the village and hill known as Hlum.

135
00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:59.990
It lay right beside Benedek's headquarters, it commanded the entire front, and at that hour it was undefended.

136
00:14:59.990 --> 00:15:11.000
The Prussian First Guard Division, under the command of Wilhelm Hiller von Gaertringen, came up the slope without meeting any resistance at all and took the hill around three o'clock.

137
00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:18.330
The Austrian batteries were struck while still trying to work out what had happened, as the first volleys came from behind their own lines.

138
00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:22.940
When Benedek was told that Hlum had fallen, he did not believe it.

139
00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:28.030
He rode forward to see it with his own eyes, and saw the Prussian Guards up on the hill.

140
00:15:28.030 --> 00:15:34.080
At that moment the battle was over; the only question left was whether the army could be saved.

141
00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:39.010
In that last hour the Austrian army showed what it had failed to show all day.

142
00:15:39.010 --> 00:15:47.780
Wilhelm von Ramming's Sixth Corps made a desperate counterattack toward the village behind Hlum; for a while they came within reach of retaking the hill.

143
00:15:47.780 --> 00:15:54.310
Hiller von Gaertringen, the commander of the Prussian Guards, died of a wound he received in that fighting.

144
00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:58.120
But the flow of arriving Prussian troops could not be stopped.

145
00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:05.610
At three o'clock Benedek gave the order to withdraw, and to make that order possible he sacrificed the two finest arms of his army.

146
00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:15.180
The Austrian reserve artillery stayed where it stood in the open ground, battery by battery, and went on firing; they did not pull back until the guns fell silent.

147
00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:21.870
Then the Austrian and Saxon cavalry charged the advancing Prussian lines one wave after another.

148
00:16:21.870 --> 00:16:26.640
In those final charges close to two thousand horsemen and horses were lost.

149
00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:33.970
What was bought in return was a few hours — and those few hours kept the Army of the North from being destroyed altogether.

150
00:16:33.970 --> 00:16:37.460
The retreat turned into a nightmare all the same.

151
00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:44.310
The army poured back toward the single way out behind it, toward the crossings over the river Elbe and the fortress of Königgrätz.

152
00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:53.560
The fortress commander, afraid that the Prussians might slip inside, kept the gates shut, and he had flooded the surrounding countryside for the sake of the defence.

153
00:16:53.560 --> 00:17:00.730
Thousands of soldiers, gun carriages and horses became entangled on a bank turned to swamp and at the narrow bridgeheads.

154
00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:03.900
By evening the figures spoke for themselves.

155
00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:08.830
On the Prussian side there were around nine thousand dead, wounded and missing.

156
00:17:08.830 --> 00:17:16.000
Austria and her ally Saxony had lost close to forty-four thousand men; roughly twenty thousand of them were prisoners.

157
00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:21.250
One hundred and eighty-seven guns and dozens of colours had been left in enemy hands.

158
00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:29.700
In a single day, in a single afternoon, one of the oldest dynastic armies in Europe had lost a third of its fighting strength.

159
00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:37.830
That evening Benedek sent his emperor a second telegram: the catastrophe he had warned of two days earlier had come to pass that day.

160
00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:43.240
At Prussian headquarters, a very different sentence was being spoken.

161
00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:53.610
When the Crown Prince's colours appeared on the ridge to the north, Moltke is said to have turned to the king and told him that his majesty had won not merely a battle but the entire campaign.

162
00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:59.180
That same day in Berlin, voting was under way for the Prussian House of Representatives.

163
00:17:59.180 --> 00:18:06.350
The liberals who had resisted Bismarck through all the years he governed without a budget fell back seriously for the first time in that election.

164
00:18:06.350 --> 00:18:10.320
He had won on the battlefield and at the ballot box on the same day.

165
00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:19.730
In the days after the victory was won, the real quarrel began inside Prussia's own headquarters, and the outcome of that quarrel determined more than the war itself did.

166
00:18:19.730 --> 00:18:22.420
The army was advancing toward Vienna.

167
00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:31.670
For King Wilhelm the First and most of his commanders the matter was self-evident: they would enter the enemy capital, hold a parade, take territory from Austria,

168
00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:35.960
and punish the German states that had sided with the Habsburgs.

169
00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:40.890
The bill for centuries of Prussian-Austrian rivalry would finally be presented.

170
00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:49.180
Bismarck stood against it alone, and found himself in a strange position: the man accused of starting the war was now trying to stop it.

171
00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:52.510
His argument was not sentimental but cold.

172
00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:57.600
Prussia's aim was not to destroy Austria but to push her out of the German world.

173
00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:07.490
Since that work was done, the Austria of tomorrow ought to be a partner rather than an enemy; a state that has been humiliated looks for revenge at the first opportunity.

174
00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:10.660
What is more, the clock was running.

175
00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:20.710
In France, Napoleon the Third had stepped onto the stage with an offer of mediation, and he was not pleased to see Prussia growing so large; within the Prussian army, meanwhile,

176
00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:24.840
it was said that cholera was carrying off more men than the fighting.

177
00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:30.730
If peace were not signed within a week, the victory already won could turn into a European crisis.

178
00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:35.660
The dispute came to a head in the Moravian town of Nikolsburg.

179
00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:43.630
In his own memoirs Bismarck describes those days as a moment when, unable to persuade the king, he walked out of the room,

180
00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:49.200
so desperate and so close to a nervous collapse that he considered throwing himself from the window.

181
00:19:49.200 --> 00:19:53.010
The man who unlocked it was the Crown Prince.

182
00:19:53.010 --> 00:20:00.180
He went in to his father, held a conversation that lasted two hours, and by the time he came out the king had given way.

183
00:20:00.180 --> 00:20:09.590
Wilhelm wrote a bitter sentence in his own hand in the margin of Bismarck's memorandum: he said that after his army's brilliant victories he was being forced to bite into this sour apple.

184
00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:20.120
An armistice was made on the twenty-second of July, the preliminary peace of Nikolsburg was signed on the twenty-sixth of July, and on the twenty-third of August, eighteen sixty-six,

185
00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:23.130
the Treaty of Prague brought the war to an end.

186
00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:27.420
The text is a textbook example of how measured a victor can be.

187
00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:36.190
Austria gave Prussia not a single inch of territory; she paid a symbolic indemnity and surrendered Venetia to Italy through the hands of Napoleon the Third.

188
00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:45.600
In return, the German Confederation, which had stood since eighteen fifteen, was dissolved, and Austria was shut out of German affairs entirely.

189
00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:54.210
Prussia annexed Hanover, Hesse-Kassel and Nassau, which had stood beside Vienna in the war, along with the city of Frankfurt and Schleswig-Holstein.

190
00:20:54.210 --> 00:21:01.540
The following year, all the German states north of the river Main were united in the North German Confederation under Prussian leadership.

191
00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:07.430
The question that had run for a century and a half — around whom would Germany gather?

192
00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.280
— had been answered in seven weeks.

193
00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:13.450
The consequences spread outward in waves.

194
00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:21.260
In Paris, "revenge for Sadowa" became a slogan; the French realised too late that a great power had been born on their own frontier,

195
00:21:21.260 --> 00:21:26.350
and when they faced that power four years later they met the machine of eighteen sixty-six.

196
00:21:26.350 --> 00:21:37.520
On the eighteenth of January, eighteen seventy-one, the German Empire was proclaimed at Versailles, and the man who took the crown was the same Wilhelm who had been forced to bite the sour apple at Nikolsburg.

197
00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:46.290
In Vienna, defeat pushed the empire into changing its internal structure: a compromise with the Hungarians was signed in eighteen sixty-seven,

198
00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:52.180
and the Austrian Empire spent the remaining half-century of its life as Austria-Hungary.

199
00:21:52.180 --> 00:22:04.470
In Prussia, the liberal opposition approved the law that, in September of eighteen sixty-six, retroactively absolved a prime minister who had governed in violation of the constitution; success had bought the principle.

200
00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:13.400
Every army in Europe took the lesson too, and for the next forty years they copied the Prussian model — the general staff, conscription and railway planning.

201
00:22:13.400 --> 00:22:17.050
A single man was left outside this new world.

202
00:22:17.050 --> 00:22:22.940
Benedek stepped down from his command on the twenty-sixth of July, eighteen sixty-six.

203
00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:33.470
A military inquiry was opened against him, then halted by the intervention of the emperor; had the case gone forward, the decisions of the army and of the court would have stood trial as well.

204
00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:43.040
In December, the empire's official paper, the Wiener Zeitung — the Vienna Gazette — published a piece laying the entire responsibility for the defeat on him alone.

205
00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:52.450
Benedek was given no right of reply; he was ordered never to utter a single word about the circumstances of the battle for the rest of his life, and he obeyed that order.

206
00:22:52.450 --> 00:23:01.380
He withdrew to Graz, kept silent for fifteen years, and died on the twenty-seventh of April, eighteen eighty-one, without military honours.

207
00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:06.630
His papers, so the story goes, were burned after his death at his own request.

208
00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:15.560
This is what Königgrätz really tells us: on the morning of the third of July, it was not two armies that faced one another, but two centuries.

209
00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:23.370
On one side, the old world — commanded by titles, prizing courage over order, carrying its news on horseback.

210
00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:30.220
On the other, a new kind of organisation, running on railway timetables, telegraph lines and shared training.

211
00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:34.350
The victor that day was not a commander, but a method.

212
00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:40.720
And because Europe had seen how quickly that method could win, it never looked at another war the same way again.

213
00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:44.370
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

214
00:23:44.370 --> 00:23:48.020
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
